<section class="phero"><div class="phero-media"><img src="/assets/img/photos/woodstove-cabin.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/woodstove-cabin-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/woodstove-cabin.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="682" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><a href="/nh-appraisal-guides/">Guides</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">Burst Pipes at the Camp: Seasonal and Vacant Property Claims in New Hampshire</span></nav><p class="kicker">Guide · 6 min read · September 23, 2026</p><h1>Burst Pipes at the Camp: Seasonal and Vacant Property Claims in New Hampshire</h1><p class="lede">Frozen pipes in a closed-up camp or an empty house are among the most common winter losses in New Hampshire, and among the most contested. The policy’s heat and vacancy wording usually decides the outcome.</p></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<p>Thousands of New Hampshire properties sit empty for part of the year: lake camps around Winnipesaukee and Squam, ski condos near Loon and Waterville Valley, inherited homes waiting for an estate to settle. When a pipe freezes and splits in one of them, water can run for days before anyone notices. These claims are common, expensive and frequently disputed, and the arguments almost always start with a few lines of policy wording.</p>
<h2>The policy language that decides these claims</h2>
<p>Most homeowners and dwelling forms treat freezing differently when nobody is living in the building. Typical conditions say freezing damage is covered only if you used reasonable care to maintain heat in the building, or shut off the water supply and drained the system and appliances. Some forms add a separate <strong>vacancy</strong> or <strong>unoccupancy</strong> clause that limits or removes coverage for certain perils once the property has been empty longer than a stated period — often 30 or 60 consecutive days.</p>
<p>Wording varies widely between insurers and policy types, and courts read it closely. Before you do anything else, find the freezing, vacancy and unoccupancy language in your policy and read it carefully. If it’s unclear how it applies to your situation, ask a New Hampshire attorney.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Seasonal isn’t the same as vacant</b>Many policies treat a furnished seasonal camp you visit regularly differently from a house with no furniture and no one coming by. Which category you fall into can change the answer, so describe your use of the property accurately when you report the claim.</div>
<h2>Evidence about heat and water</h2>
<p>When the question is whether reasonable care was taken, records matter more than recollections. Gather what you can:</p>
<ul>
<li>Fuel delivery records for oil or propane, showing the tank wasn’t run dry.</li>
<li>Electric bills for the months before the loss, which can show whether heat was running.</li>
<li>Thermostat history or remote temperature monitor alerts, if you have them.</li>
<li>Invoices from a caretaker, plumber or winterizing service.</li>
<li>Photos of the thermostat setting and the main water shutoff as you found them.</li>
</ul>
<p>Write down a simple timeline as well: the last date someone was at the property, the last fuel delivery, the date the damage was found and who found it. Insurers often ask these questions early, sometimes in a recorded statement, and a written timeline prepared from your records keeps your answers consistent and accurate. If you aren’t sure of a date, say so rather than guessing.</p>
<p>If a furnace failed, keep the failed part and the service report. A sudden mechanical failure can be a very different story from a building that was never heated.</p>
<h2>First steps when you find the damage</h2>
<ol>
<li>Shut off the main water valve and, if water reached wiring, the electricity.</li>
<li>Photograph and video everything before cleanup, including the split pipe.</li>
<li>Call a water mitigation company; most policies expect prompt steps to limit further damage.</li>
<li>Report the claim in writing and keep a log of every conversation.</li>
<li>Save the damaged pipe section and fitting.</li>
</ol>
<h2>Where the estimate tends to come up short</h2>
<p>Once coverage is accepted, the arguments shift to scope. Camps and older homes have quirks that standard estimates miss: knotty pine paneling that can’t be patched to match, plank floors that cup across whole rooms, crawlspaces with wet insulation and vapor barrier, and well pumps, pressure tanks or water heaters damaged by freezing. Long drying times in a cold building and the cost of getting crews to remote towns may also be underestimated. Mold that develops before discovery may fall under a sublimit, so check that part of the policy.</p>
<h2>Making the claim easier next winter</h2>
<p>Whatever happens with this claim, a few habits help later: a low-temperature alarm that texts you, a documented winterizing checklist, fuel on automatic delivery, and a neighbor or caretaker who checks in on a schedule. Keep the records — they are the best evidence you’ll have if it happens again.</p>
<p>If coverage is accepted but the numbers don’t match the damage, our <a href="/nh-appraisal-services/pipe-burst-water-appraisal/">pipe burst and water appraisal</a> service can help, starting with a review of the insurer’s estimate. For Lakes Region properties, see our <a href="/new-hampshire-cities/laconia-nh/">Laconia area page</a>, and use the <a href="/nh-claim-calculators/nh-claim-deadline-calendar/">deadline calendar</a> to keep track of policy dates.</p>
